<legalnotice>
<para>Sonic the Hedgehog and its characters are trademarks of SEGA. This handbook describes an unofficial PC
recompilation of the 1991 game.</para>
</legalnotice>

<date>2026-10-02</date>
<releaseinfo>1.0</releaseinfo>

<abstract>
<para>Sonic 1 PC is Sonic the Hedgehog (1991, Mega Drive/Genesis) as a native program: the same game, in a window with a menu bar, a
picture that can change shape, modern audio and a set of tools for looking inside it. This handbook tells you how to play it,
what is waiting for you in every zone and special stage, how to set up the picture, the sound and the controls, and what the
secrets are.</para>
</abstract>

<keywordset>
<keyword>Sonic</keyword>
<keyword>Sonic the Hedgehog</keyword>
<keyword>platform game</keyword>
<keyword>Mega Drive</keyword>
<keyword>Genesis</keyword>
</keywordset>
</bookinfo>


<!-- ============================================================ -->
<chapter id="introduction">
<title>Crush Dr. Robotnik!</title>

<para>South Island has a villain, and he has a plan. Dr. Ivo Robotnik is rounding up the island's animals, sealing them
inside robot bodies and combing every zone for the six Chaos Emeralds. The only one fast enough to stop him is Sonic: a
hedgehog with a taste for speed, a spiky attitude and a very good pair of sneakers.</para>

<para>Your job is to run, roll and spin through six zones, smash Robotnik's robots (the badniks) to set the animals free,
grab rings to stay in one piece, and face the doctor himself at the end of each zone. Find the secret special stages and
you may get your hands on the Chaos Emeralds as well. Ready? Be quick, be bold, be Sonic.</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The title screen</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/title.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The title screen: Sonic in front of the winged ring, with PRESS START BUTTON underneath.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>This is the real 1991 game, running as a native program on your computer. Its rules, its physics and its quirks are
the cartridge's, kept as they were unless there was a good reason to change them. What is new is everything around the
game:</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>The picture can be the original shape or one of four others, in a window or full screen
(<xref linkend="resolution"/>).</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Music and sound effects have separate volumes, and every sound chip channel can be muted
(<xref linkend="audio"/>).</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>The keyboard and any gamepad can be set up the way you like, including how far a worn stick has to move
before it counts (<xref linkend="controls"/>).</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Sonic can charge a spin dash, the ending and the credits are complete, and so is the continue screen.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>A few of the original's bugs are fixed: you cannot walk-jump off a monitor or a wall with the wrong pose any
more, TIME flashes at nine minutes whether or not you have rings (the later games did that too), and the game-over music
plays.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>A debug mode opens windows onto the game's video memory, sound chips, objects and variables, with a console,
a demo recorder and the SMPS Inspector (<xref linkend="tools"/>).</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>New to the game? Read on from <xref linkend="getting-started"/>. Know it by heart? Jump to
<xref linkend="resolution"/> for the picture settings or <xref linkend="cheats"/> for the secrets.</para>
</chapter>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<chapter id="getting-started">
<title>Starting Up</title>

<sect1 id="starting">
<title>Start the game</title>

<para>Run <command>Sonic</command>. The window opens on the SEGA screen, then the title screen. Press
<keycap>Return</keycap> (or <guibutton>Start</guibutton> on a gamepad) to begin a new game in Green Hill Zone. Leave the
title screen alone for a few moments and a demonstration game plays; press Start to return to the title, and Start again
to begin.</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The main window</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/mainwindow.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The main window: the menu bar above the picture and the status bar below it.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>The menu bar sits inside the window. <guimenu>File</guimenu>, <guimenu>Audio</guimenu>, <guimenu>Settings</guimenu>
and <guimenu>Help</guimenu> are always there; <guimenu>View</guimenu> and <guimenu>Tools</guimenu> appear while the debug
tools are available (<xref linkend="debug-mode"/>). The right-hand side of the status bar lists the tools that are
running, so that none of them goes unnoticed. <keycap>F1</keycap> opens this handbook.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="controls">
<title>Take Control!</title>

<para>Learn the buttons before you start: everything Sonic does comes from a direction pad and three buttons. The
keyboard and a gamepad can be used at the same time.</para>

<informaltable>
<tgroup cols="3">
<thead>
<row><entry>Mega Drive pad</entry><entry>Keyboard</entry><entry>Gamepad</entry></row>
</thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry>D-pad</entry><entry><keycap>Arrow keys</keycap> or <keycap>W</keycap> <keycap>A</keycap> <keycap>S</keycap> <keycap>D</keycap></entry><entry>D-pad or left stick</entry></row>
<row><entry>A</entry><entry><keycap>B</keycap></entry><entry>X</entry></row>
<row><entry>B</entry><entry><keycap>N</keycap></entry><entry>A</entry></row>
<row><entry>C</entry><entry><keycap>M</keycap></entry><entry>B</entry></row>
<row><entry>Start</entry><entry><keycap>Return</keycap></entry><entry>Start</entry></row>
<row><entry>Fullscreen</entry><entry><keycap>F11</keycap></entry><entry>-</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</informaltable>

<para>The buttons can be changed in <menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Configure Sonic the
Hedgehog...</guimenuitem></menuchoice>. Click a button in the table, then press the key (or, in the gamepad column, the
gamepad button) you want; <keycap>Escape</keycap> cancels. Every Mega Drive button has two keyboard keys, and a key can
drive only one button (assigning it elsewhere takes it away from the old one). The D-pad and left stick always move
Sonic on a gamepad. <keycap>F11</keycap> and the backtick key are reserved. <guibutton>Restore Defaults</guibutton> puts back
the table above. The choices are saved with the other settings.</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The controls settings</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/controls.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Configure Sonic the Hedgehog window listing each pad button with its keys.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>The <guilabel>Gamepad</guilabel> tab of the same window sets how far a stick has to move before it counts: a
<emphasis>dead zone</emphasis> for the left stick, which moves Sonic, and one for the right stick, which only the debug
tools use. A stick that is worn and drifts can make Sonic creep along on its own; raise its dead zone until the dot in the
picture stays inside the shaded square while the stick is at rest. This does what Steam Input's dead zone would, without
needing it. Each stick has a picture and a readout showing where it is as you move it, as a percentage of its
travel, and whether that counts as a push or is inside the dead zone.</para>

<para>The dead zone has two shapes. A <guilabel>Box</guilabel> judges each direction on its own: the stick has to pass the
line on that axis. A <guilabel>Ring</guilabel> judges the stick's overall push, in any direction: drift that sits a little
off to one side stays ignored, and once the stick is pushed past the ring, the push is read as one of eight directions. The
shaded area in the picture changes with the choice.</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The gamepad settings</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/controls-gamepad.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Gamepad tab: a view of each stick with its dead zone shaded, and a slider for each.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>


<para>What the buttons do:</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>D-pad</term>
<listitem><para>Left and right move Sonic, and holding a direction makes him speed up. Standing still, press up or down to
look at the top or bottom of the screen (it will not scroll further than the level goes). Pressing down while you run rolls
him into a ball.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Button A, B or C</term>
<listitem><para>Any of the three makes Sonic jump; hold it for a higher jump. A jump is a spin attack too, so it is also
how you land on a badnik.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Start</term>
<listitem><para>Starts the game, and pauses it. Press it again to carry on. While the game is paused, holding
<guibutton>B</guibutton> or pressing <guibutton>C</guibutton> steps a single frame.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<sect2 id="stunts">
<title>Sonic's super stunts</title>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>Press down while Sonic is running to roll up into the <emphasis>spin attack</emphasis>: a rolling Sonic
bumps badniks out of the way instead of getting hurt by them.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Press a jump button while rolling to jump with the spin attack still on.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Standing still, press down to duck.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="spin-dash">
<title>The spin dash</title>
<para>Duck with <keycap>Down</keycap>, then press a jump button to start charging a spin dash. Each further press winds it
up more and raises the pitch of the revving sound. Release <keycap>Down</keycap> to shoot off. (This is Sonic 2's move, added
here: the cartridge's Sonic cannot do it.)</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="level-select">
<title>The secret level select</title>

<para>The level select is hidden. At the title screen press <keycap>Up</keycap>, <keycap>Down</keycap>,
<keycap>Left</keycap>, <keycap>Right</keycap> (a ring sound tells you it worked), then hold
<guibutton>A</guibutton> and press <guibutton>Start</guibutton>.</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The level select</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/levelselect.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The level select: the zones and acts in a list, the highlighted row in yellow, and SOUND SELECT at the bottom.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>Move the highlight with <keycap>Up</keycap> and <keycap>Down</keycap> and choose a row with any of A, B,
C or <guibutton>Start</guibutton>. The two last rows are the Special Stage and the sound test: on SOUND SELECT,
<keycap>Left</keycap> and <keycap>Right</keycap> change the number and a button plays that sound. The highlighted
row and the sound number are remembered until you quit the game.</para>

<para>A level chosen from the level select keeps your special stage number and your emeralds, just like entering a
level through a giant ring, and the special stage numbers go on from where they were.</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<chapter id="playing">
<title>Playing the Game</title>

<sect1 id="goal">
<title>Reach for the Rings!</title>

<para>The island has six zones, and every zone has three acts. Dash to the signpost at the end of an act to finish it; the
third act of each zone ends in a fight with Dr. Robotnik instead. Along the way you will dodge traps and robots, so keep
your eyes open for <emphasis>rings</emphasis>.</para>

<para>Rings are your armour. While you hold any, something that hurts Sonic only knocks them all out of his hands (you
can chase some of them down and pick them up again). With none left, the next hit costs a life. A spin attack protects
you from badniks but not from things that crush you, and nothing protects you from falling down a pit or drowning. Every
100 rings earn a life, and so does every 50,000 points.</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Green Hill Zone</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/gameplay.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Sonic playing Green Hill Zone, the score, time and rings in the top left corner and the lives in the bottom left.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<sect2 id="hud">
<title>Keeping count</title>
<para>The counters on the screen tell you how you are doing:</para>
<orderedlist>
<listitem><para><emphasis>SCORE:</emphasis> your score so far.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>TIME:</emphasis> how long the act has taken. You have ten minutes for each act: go over, and TIME
OVER costs you a life. It is game time, not clock time, so it stops while the game is paused, and TIME starts flashing
once nine minutes have passed.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>RINGS:</emphasis> the rings you hold. The word flashes when you have none.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>SONIC &times;:</emphasis> the lives you have left, in the bottom left corner (the top right
corner when the picture is one of the wider shapes).</para></listitem>
</orderedlist>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="lampposts">
<title>Lampposts</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>A lamppost</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/checkpoint.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>A lamppost.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>Lampposts stand in every zone. Run past one and it remembers where you got to, along with your score and time. If
you lose a life you restart from the last lamppost you touched, without your rings but with that score and time (a TIME
OVER clears the remembered time, so you cannot get stuck restarting with the clock almost out).</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="monitors">
<title>Items for Survival</title>

<para>Jump on a video monitor to smash it open; what is inside helps you (mostly).</para>

<informaltable>
<tgroup cols="3">
<thead>
<row><entry>Monitor</entry><entry>Item</entry><entry>What it does</entry></row>
</thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/monitor-super_ring.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Super Ring monitor</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Super Ring</entry><entry>Gives you ten rings at once.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/monitor-shield.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Shield monitor</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Shield</entry><entry>Takes a hit for you, so you keep your rings, and still protects you when you have none. It does nothing against being crushed, falling or drowning.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/monitor-power_sneakers.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Power Sneakers monitor</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Power Sneakers</entry><entry>Makes you run faster for a while (the music speeds up too).</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/monitor-invincible.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Invincibility monitor</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Invincible</entry><entry>Stars circle Sonic and enemies cannot hurt him, for a short while. Crushing, falling and drowning still can.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/monitor-1up.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Extra life monitor</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>One-Up</entry><entry>One more life. (Collecting 100 rings gives you one too.)</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/monitor-robotnik.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Robotnik monitor</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Robotnik</entry><entry>A trap. It hurts Sonic instead of helping him.</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</informaltable>

<para>Other things you will run into:</para>

<informaltable>
<tgroup cols="2">
<tbody>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/item-ring.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>A ring</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>A <emphasis>ring</emphasis>: collect as many as you can.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/item-special_stage_ring.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>A giant ring</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>A <emphasis>giant ring</emphasis>: hidden in some acts, it shows itself only while you carry 50 or more rings, and opens the way to a special stage (see <xref linkend="special-stages"/>).</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/signpost.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>The signpost</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>The <emphasis>signpost</emphasis> at the end of an act: run past it to finish the act and collect your bonuses.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/capsule.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>The animal capsule</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>The <emphasis>capsule</emphasis> that appears when a boss is beaten: jump on its button to set the animals free.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/switch.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>A switch</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>A <emphasis>switch</emphasis>: stand on it to press it. It opens doors, lowers pillars and so on.</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</informaltable>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="zones">
<title>Zip Through the Zones!</title>

<para>Six zones, three acts each. Every zone has its own look, its own traps and its own cast of badniks, and the third act
ends with a boss: Dr. Robotnik at the controls of a different machine each time. The zones come in this order.</para>

<sect2 id="zone-ghz">
<title>1. Green Hill Zone</title>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Green Hill Zone</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/green_hill_zone.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Green Hill Zone: palm trees, a lake and a checkered cliff.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
<para>Sun, palm trees and waterfalls: the friendliest place on the island, and the best one to learn the ropes in. Run
around the loops, tumble through tunnels, and look for the faster, higher routes above the main path.</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Terrain:</emphasis> loops and tunnels you can take at full speed, wooden bridges that sag under
Sonic, swinging platforms, ledges that crumble a moment after you land on them, and rock walls you can smash with a spin
attack.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Hazards:</emphasis> plenty of spikes (jump them, never land on them) and springs that throw you
a long way.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Badniks:</emphasis> Moto Bug, Crabmeat, Buzz Bomber, Chopper and Newtron.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Boss (Act 3):</emphasis> Robotnik flies back and forth with a heavy checkered ball swinging
beneath his machine. Dodge the ball and jump on the machine; it takes eight hits.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="zone-mz">
<title>2. Marble Zone</title>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Marble Zone</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/marble_zone.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Marble Zone: ancient brick ruins above a pool of lava.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
<para>Ancient ruins, built over pools of lava. The route goes over, under and through the stonework, so slow down and think:
this zone is more puzzle than sprint.</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Terrain:</emphasis> green stone blocks you can push (shove one onto the lava and it floats along for a
while before it sinks), bricks that wobble above the lava and drop when you come near, pillars that rise when you stand on
a switch, grassy slabs, and blocks that break when you hit them.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Hazards:</emphasis> lava itself (touching it hurts), balls of fire that jump out of it, geysers,
heavy weights hung on chains that pound the floor, spikes, and in places a wall of lava that creeps after you.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Badniks:</emphasis> Buzz Bomber, Batbrain and Caterkiller.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Boss (Act 3):</emphasis> Robotnik cruises above the lava in a heated machine and drops fire onto the
platforms below. Keep out from under it and hit it whenever it comes within reach.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="zone-syz">
<title>3. Spring Yard Zone</title>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Spring Yard Zone</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/spring_yard_zone.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Spring Yard Zone: a city of round bumpers and springs at dusk.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
<para>A neon city that plays like a pinball table. Round bumpers fling Sonic every which way, springs fire him across
the screen, and there is more of the zone off the main path than on it. Look out for the giant rings: this zone hides the
most.</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Terrain:</emphasis> bumpers, springs by the dozen, blocks that move in set patterns, platforms and
spinning searchlights in the background.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Hazards:</emphasis> big spiked balls, balls swinging on chains, and spikes.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Badniks:</emphasis> Crabmeat, Buzz Bomber, Roller and Spikes (Yadrin).</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Boss (Act 3):</emphasis> Robotnik's machine carries a spiked claw that comes down onto the blocks
in the arena floor, lifts one, shakes it and lets it go. Keep out from under a falling block and strike the machine while the
claw is busy.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="zone-lz">
<title>4. Labyrinth Zone</title>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Labyrinth Zone</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/labyrinth_zone.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Labyrinth Zone: a sandstone maze, the lower half under water.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
<para>A flooded maze of carved stone. Much of it is under water, where Sonic moves slowly and, more to the point, cannot
breathe. Learn the water first, the rest comes after.</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Air:</emphasis> Sonic can hold his breath for about half a minute. Warning chimes sound as it runs
down, and in the last twelve seconds the music changes and numbers count 5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0 up from Sonic's head. If you
reach zero he drowns. Air bubbles drifting up from vents give you a gulp of air at once: stand in one and breathe.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Terrain:</emphasis> blocks that sink or rise when you stand on them, cork blocks that bob on the
water, platforms riding on rope-and-wheel conveyors (they loop round, carrying you with them), doors that switches open, flap doors that swing on a timer, waterfalls and rapids.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Hazards:</emphasis> harpoons that stab out of the walls on a timer, spikes, spiked balls, and stone
gargoyle heads that spit fire.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Badniks:</emphasis> Jaws, Burrobot and Orbinaut.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Boss (Act 3):</emphasis> there is no arena. Robotnik flies up a long shaft and you chase him, hitting
him as you climb. He pauses at the top until you catch up, then escapes to the right.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="zone-slz">
<title>5. Star Light Zone</title>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Star Light Zone</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/star_light_zone.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Star Light Zone: a girder highway above a night sky of stars.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
<para>A highway in the sky, thick with moving parts and Robotnik's bombs. It is full of things that move you about, and
the trick is to let them.</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Terrain:</emphasis> seesaws that launch you when something heavy lands on the other end, lifts,
platforms that circle on arms, staircases whose blocks step out of the wall, big fans that blow you upward, springs,
floors that fall away behind you, and girders that pass in front of the picture.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Badniks:</emphasis> Bomb and Orbinaut.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Boss (Act 3):</emphasis> Robotnik drops exploding spiked balls onto the seesaws. Use a seesaw to
launch yourself at his machine, and keep clear of the balls when they go off.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="zone-sbz">
<title>6. Scrap Brain Zone</title>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Scrap Brain Zone</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/scrap_brain_zone.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Scrap Brain Zone: purple brick walls, pipes and a window onto a flowing channel.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
<para>Robotnik's own factory, one step from his hideout, and it holds every trap he could build. The factory is the
toughest of the six zones.</para>
<note><para>Scrap Brain Zone was not always called that. During development it was Clock Work Zone, and the font of the title
card had no W, so the card read CLOCK ORK ZONE.</para></note>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Terrain:</emphasis> conveyor belts and spinning discs that carry you, floors that spin or
vanish, doors, teleporters that send you to another part of the factory, and moving blocks.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Hazards:</emphasis> circular saws, flamethrowers, electric zappers, stompers, spiked balls, and spikes.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Badniks:</emphasis> Ball Hog, Caterkiller, Bomb and Orbinaut. Act 3 is flooded and brings the
water back, with Jaws and Burrobot.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
<para>Beat the three acts and you reach <emphasis>Final Zone</emphasis>, where Robotnik waits in a private arena. He hides inside
cylinders and fires plasma balls at you, so keep moving. Defeat him there and the ending begins
(<xref linkend="ending"/>).</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="enemies">
<title>Robotnik's Badniks</title>

<para>Robotnik's robots are called <emphasis>badniks</emphasis>, and each one has an animal trapped inside. Jump on one, or
hit it with the spin attack, to smash it and free the animal; touching one any other way hurts Sonic. Each badnik is worth
at least 100 points, and the boss at the end of a zone is worth 1,000. Here is who is waiting for you, and where (the
Japanese names are in brackets).</para>

<informaltable>
<tgroup cols="4">
<thead>
<row><entry>Badnik</entry><entry>Name</entry><entry>Found in</entry><entry>Behaviour</entry></row>
</thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/badnik-crabmeat.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Crabmeat</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Crabmeat (Ganigani in Japan)</entry><entry>Green Hill, Spring Yard</entry><entry>Walks sideways and shoots two fireballs, one to each side.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/badnik-buzz_bomber.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Buzz Bomber</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Buzz Bomber (Beeton in Japan)</entry><entry>Green Hill, Marble, Spring Yard</entry><entry>Flies back and forth and fires a shot downwards when you pass beneath it.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/badnik-moto_bug.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Moto Bug</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Moto Bug (Motora in Japan)</entry><entry>Green Hill</entry><entry>Rolls along the ground, turning around now and then.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/badnik-newtron.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Newtron</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Newtron (Meleon in Japan)</entry><entry>Green Hill</entry><entry>Appears on the wall, then either fires a shot or drops to the ground and speeds off.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/badnik-chopper.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Chopper</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Chopper (Batabata in Japan)</entry><entry>Green Hill</entry><entry>A piranha that leaps out of the water again and again.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/badnik-caterkiller.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Caterkiller</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Caterkiller (Nal in Japan)</entry><entry>Marble, Scrap Brain</entry><entry>A spiked caterpillar. Only its head can be destroyed; its body hurts you.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/badnik-batbrain.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Batbrain</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Batbrain (Basaran in Japan)</entry><entry>Marble</entry><entry>Hangs from the ceiling until you come near, then swoops after you.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/badnik-burrobot.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Burrobot</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Burrobot (Mogurin in Japan)</entry><entry>Labyrinth, Scrap Brain Act 3</entry><entry>A drill-nosed mole that hops along the ground.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/badnik-jaws.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Jaws</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Jaws (Puku-puku in Japan)</entry><entry>Labyrinth, Scrap Brain Act 3</entry><entry>A piranha that swims back and forth underwater.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/badnik-orbinaut.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Orbinaut</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Orbinaut (Unidis for the blue one, Uni Uni for the green one, in Japan)</entry><entry>Labyrinth, Star Light, Scrap Brain</entry><entry>Is circled by four spiked orbs, which it may fire at you.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/enemy-bomb.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Bomb</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Bomb (Bomu in Japan)</entry><entry>Star Light, Scrap Brain</entry><entry>Walks toward you, then stops and blows up, throwing shrapnel in four directions.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/badnik-roller.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Roller</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Roller (Arma in Japan)</entry><entry>Spring Yard</entry><entry>An armadillo that curls up and rolls at you, then uncurls to run.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/badnik-spikes.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Spikes, the badnik</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Spikes (Yadrin in Japan)</entry><entry>Spring Yard</entry><entry>Paces back and forth, pausing for a second at each end. Its back is spiked, so jumping on it hurts: destroy it by rolling into it, or hit it from the side.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/badnik-ballhog.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Ball Hog</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Ball Hog (Ton-ton in Japan)</entry><entry>Scrap Brain</entry><entry>Throws bouncing bombs at you.</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</informaltable>

<note><para>If you have the manual that came in the box, you may find a different zone listed for some of these. Those placements are
holdovers from a build made after the prototype that survives and before the first release, one that has never turned up (it is
not the only lost build: leftovers in the game's code point to several earlier ones). By then the zones already had their final
names (Spring Yard Zone had been Sparkling Zone before that), but the badniks had not yet been placed as they were in the end. The
code remembers too: the Spikes badnik (Yadrin) is marked there as meant for Marble Zone as well, and the Caterkiller's art still
carries the name of Spring Yard. The table above is where each one is placed in the finished game.</para></note>

<para>Some hazards cannot be destroyed. <emphasis>Spikes</emphasis> hurt whenever you touch their points (jump on them
at your peril), and a <emphasis>spring</emphasis> throws you in the direction it points.</para>

<informaltable>
<tgroup cols="2">
<tbody>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/spikes.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Spikes</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Spikes, standing up, hanging down or on the side of a wall.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/spring.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>A spring</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>A spring: red ones are strong, yellow ones are stronger still.</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</informaltable>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="special-stages">
<title>The Secret Zone: Special Stages</title>

<para>Rings are good for more than armour. Carry <emphasis>50 or more</emphasis> to a giant ring and it glitters and lets
you jump in: you are whisked away to a <emphasis>special stage</emphasis>, a secret place where Sonic is a rolling ball
inside a big, slowly rotating maze, with a Chaos Emerald hidden somewhere in it. There are six special stages, one for each
emerald. Giant rings are hidden in some acts of every zone except Scrap Brain; once you hold all six emeralds, they stop
appearing.</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>A special stage</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/special.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>A special stage: the maze of blocks rotating over the bird background.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<sect2 id="ss-rules">
<title>How it works</title>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>The maze turns steadily around Sonic. Steer with left and right, jump with A, B or C, and use the blocks
to bounce your way through. There is no clock in here: take your time.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>The stage is a trap, and there are exactly two ways out. <emphasis>Touch the Chaos Emerald</emphasis> to win
it, or <emphasis>touch a GOAL block</emphasis> to leave without it (that is how you fail a stage). Either way you go back
to the zone you came from.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Stages come in turn: if you fail, the next attempt is the next stage, and stages whose emerald you already
hold are skipped.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Rings in the maze count too. Leave with 50 or more rings and the first time you do it you earn a
<emphasis>continue</emphasis> (<xref linkend="continues"/>). The results screen adds 100 points for every ring you have.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Look out for <emphasis>ghost blocks</emphasis>: parts of the wall that you can pass through once, but which
turn into solid red-and-white blocks behind you. They can trap you.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Six emeralds, six colours: blue, yellow, pink, green, red and white. Collect all six and the ending changes
(<xref linkend="ending"/>).</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="ss-blocks">
<title>Blocks in the Secret Zone</title>

<para>These are the blocks you will meet in the maze:</para>

<informaltable>
<tgroup cols="3">
<thead>
<row><entry>Block</entry><entry>Name</entry><entry>What it does</entry></row>
</thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/spec-wall.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Wall</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Wall</entry><entry>Solid. Sonic cannot pass through it.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/spec-jump_block.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Jump block</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Jump block</entry><entry>A solid red and white block. Stand on it and jump off it like a wall.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/spec-bumper.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Bumper</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Bumper</entry><entry>Throws Sonic away from it.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/spec-up.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>UP block</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>UP</entry><entry>Speeds up the rotation of the maze. It then turns into a DOWN block.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/spec-down.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>DOWN block</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>DOWN</entry><entry>Slows the rotation back down. It then turns into an UP block.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/spec-reverse.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>R block</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>R</entry><entry>Reverses the direction of the rotation.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/spec-diamond.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Glass block</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Glass block</entry><entry>Breaks after being touched several times, changing colour as it weakens.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/spec-goal.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>GOAL block</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>GOAL</entry><entry>Touching it ends the stage, and you leave without the emerald. It is the way you fail, so keep away from it.</entry></row>
<row><entry><inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/spec-emerald.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject><textobject><phrase>Chaos Emerald</phrase></textobject></inlinemediaobject></entry><entry>Chaos Emerald</entry><entry>Touch it to win the stage. Collecting it plays the emerald fanfare.</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</informaltable>

<note><para>The special stage music never speeds up (the original did not either).</para></note>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="ss-tips">
<title>Hints</title>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>The maze starts slow. Do not touch an UP block until you are ready for it: the faster maze gives you less time
to line up a jump.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Know where the GOAL blocks are before you start bouncing around: they are easy to hit by accident, and they end the stage.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>An UP block you have used turns into a DOWN block (and the other way round), so you can always bring the
speed back.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>An R block reverses the turning, which turns a bad approach into a good one: use it to change your angle.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Glass blocks give way after a few touches, changing colour as they weaken: hit one over and over to open a
shortcut.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="game-over">
<title>Game Over, Continues and Score</title>

<para>You start with three lives. Lose them all and the game is over: GAME OVER sounds its jingle and you are sent back to
the SEGA screen, unless you have a continue.</para>

<sect2 id="continues">
<title>Continues</title>
<para>Collecting 50 rings in a special stage earns a <emphasis>continue</emphasis>. When you lose your last life and
have one, the <guilabel>CONTINUE</guilabel> screen appears: Sonic lies on the floor while a countdown runs from ten.
The little Sonics above the number show how many continues you have (the one you are about to use is not shown).
Press <guibutton>Start</guibutton> before the countdown reaches zero and Sonic gets up and runs off, and you start the
act again with three lives; your rings, time and score begin from nothing. If the countdown runs out the game is over
and returns to the SEGA screen.</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The continue screen</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/continue.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The continue screen: the word CONTINUE, three little Sonics, the countdown and Sonic lying on the floor.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="scoring">
<title>Scoring</title>
<para>Each badnik you smash is worth at least 100 points, and beating Dr. Robotnik at the end of a zone is worth 1,000. When
you finish an act, two bonuses are added to your score: a <emphasis>Time Bonus</emphasis>, bigger the faster you were, and a
<emphasis>Ring Bonus</emphasis>, 100 points for every ring you still have. Every 50,000 points gives you an extra life.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<chapter id="ending">
<title>The Ending and the Credits</title>

<para>Defeat Dr. Robotnik in the Final Zone and the ending begins: Sonic runs back to the animals in Green Hill.
With all six Chaos Emeralds he holds them up, they spin, the screen flashes white and flowers bloom everywhere;
without them he simply leaps at the screen. Either way the SONIC THE HEDGEHOG logo slides in and the credits
follow.</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The ending</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/ending.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The ending: Sonic stopped among the animals in Green Hill Zone.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The logo</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/ending-logo.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Sonic leaping at the screen as the SONIC THE HEDGEHOG logo slides in.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>If you wrecked Dr. Robotnik's ship as he fled the Final Zone, you can see it fall in the distant background
of the ending while Sonic runs: a long-forgotten graphic that the original game loaded and never used. It
appears with either ending.</para>

<sect1 id="credits">
<title>The credits</title>
<para>The credits show a page of staff for two seconds and then play a short demonstration of one of the zones
(Green Hill, Marble, Spring Yard, Labyrinth, Star Light, Scrap Brain, twice, and Green Hill again) before the next
page.</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>A credits page</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/credits.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The first credits page, SONIC TEAM STAFF.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>The credits end on one of two screens: <guilabel>END</guilabel> if Sonic has all six Chaos Emeralds, with
Robotnik raging, or <guilabel>TRY AGAIN</guilabel> if not, with Robotnik juggling the emeralds you did not find.
<guibutton>Start</guibutton> (or thirty seconds) returns to the SEGA screen.</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The TRY AGAIN screen</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/tryagain.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Robotnik juggling the missing emeralds above the words TRY AGAIN.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The END screen</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/theend.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The final END screen, with Robotnik jumping with rage.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<chapter id="resolution">
<title>Pick Your Picture: Resolution and Full Screen</title>

<para>The original game draws a 320 &times; 224 picture (an aspect ratio of 10:7) that a television stretches to
fit. Sonic 1 PC can draw the picture in other shapes, adding extra screen where the game has room for it rather
than stretching what is there.</para>

<sect1 id="resolution-menu">
<title>The Resolution menu</title>

<para>Open <menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guisubmenu>Resolution</guisubmenu></menuchoice> and choose the
shape of the picture.</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Resolution menu</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/menu-resolution.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The File menu with its Resolution submenu open: Original, 16:9, 8:5, 5:4 and 4:3.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<informaltable>
<tgroup cols="3">
<thead>
<row><entry>Choice</entry><entry>Picture</entry><entry>What changes</entry></row>
</thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry><guimenuitem>Original (10:7)</guimenuitem></entry><entry>320 &times; 224</entry><entry>The picture of the Mega Drive.</entry></row>
<row><entry><guimenuitem>16:9</guimenuitem></entry><entry>400 &times; 224</entry><entry>Wider: you see 80 pixels more of the level, and the lives counter moves to the upper right corner.</entry></row>
<row><entry><guimenuitem>8:5</guimenuitem></entry><entry>360 &times; 224</entry><entry>Wider (the 16:10 of many monitors), with the lives counter in the upper right.</entry></row>
<row><entry><guimenuitem>5:4</guimenuitem></entry><entry>320 &times; 256</entry><entry>Taller: you see 32 more rows.</entry></row>
<row><entry><guimenuitem>4:3</guimenuitem></entry><entry>320 &times; 240</entry><entry>Taller: you see 16 more rows.</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</informaltable>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>16:9</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/gameplay-169.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Green Hill Zone in 16:9: the picture is wider and the lives counter is in the upper right corner.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>4:3</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/gameplay-43.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Green Hill Zone in 4:3: the picture is taller.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>In a window, choosing a resolution also resizes the window so that the picture keeps its (doubled) size.
Full screen only uses the aspect ratio: the window stays the size of your screen and the picture is scaled to fit
it, with black bars where the shapes differ. The choice is remembered for next time.</para>

<para>The demonstration games (on the title screen and between the credits pages) play in whichever shape you chose, and play out just as they were recorded.</para>

<para>A new resolution takes hold at once during a level, where the picture is redrawn around the camera. On other
screens (the title screen, for instance) it takes hold when the next screen starts.</para>

<tip><para>The command line option <option>--resolution</option> <replaceable>N</replaceable> starts the game in
one of the shapes without changing the saved choice: 0 is the original, 1 is 16:9, 2 is 8:5, 3 is 5:4 and 4 is
4:3.</para></tip>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="fullscreen">
<title>Full screen</title>
<para>Press <keycap>F11</keycap> or choose <menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Fullscreen</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
to switch between a window and full screen. The menu bar and status bar are hidden in full screen; press
<keycap>F11</keycap> again to bring them back.</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<chapter id="audio">
<title>Turn It Up! Audio</title>

<para>The <guimenu>Audio</guimenu> menu holds a switch and a volume slider for the music and another pair for the
sound effects, and a <guisubmenu>Channels</guisubmenu> submenu that lets you silence individual sound chip channels
(the six FM channels, the three PSG tone channels, the noise channel and the DAC, which plays the sampled drums). A channel is audible
while its entry is checked; <guimenuitem>Unmute All</guimenuitem> turns them all back on. The settings are remembered
between runs.</para>
</chapter>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<chapter id="cheats">
<title>Secrets, Cheats and Easter Eggs</title>

<sect1 id="debug-mode">
<title>The debug cheat</title>

<para>At the title screen enter <keycap>C</keycap>, <keycap>C</keycap>, <keycap>C</keycap>, <keycap>C</keycap>,
<keycap>Up</keycap>, <keycap>Down</keycap>, <keycap>Left</keycap>, <keycap>Right</keycap> (you hear a ring). The
<guimenu>View</guimenu> and <guimenu>Tools</guimenu> menus appear, the console can be opened, and in a level you can
press <guibutton>B</guibutton> to leave Sonic's body and fly around placing objects: <keycap>C</keycap> places the
selected object, <guibutton>A</guibutton> selects the next one, <keycap>/</keycap> the previous one and
<keycap>,</keycap> <keycap>.</keycap> (or <keycap>[</keycap> <keycap>]</keycap>) change its subtype;
<guibutton>B</guibutton> again turns back into Sonic. Special stages and the ending have object lists of their
own.</para>

<para>The same code also switches on the hidden Japanese credits described below.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="japanese-credits">
<title>The Japanese credits</title>
<para>With the debug cheat entered earlier in the session, hold <guibutton>A</guibutton>, <guibutton>B</guibutton>,
<guibutton>C</guibutton> and <keycap>Down</keycap> together while the title sequence starts with the SONIC TEAM
PRESENTS screen: the Japanese staff credits are shown instead.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="easter-eggs">
<title>The Easter Eggs menu</title>

<para>The sound test on the level select has two more numbers after its last sound: <guilabel>9E</guilabel> and
<guilabel>9F</guilabel>. (In the original game, once a cheat was on, they started the credits and the ending directly.) Step to
them with <keycap>Right</keycap>: the numbers between the last real sound and 9E are empty slots that play nothing, so keep
going. From 00, <keycap>Left</keycap> wraps round to 9F in one press. No cheat code is needed.</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The sound test at 9E</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/soundtest.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The level select with SOUND SELECT on 9E.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>Press a button on either of them and the Easter Eggs menu opens.</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Easter Eggs menu</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/eastereggs.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Easter Eggs menu: Credits, Good Ending, Bad Ending, Wrecked Ship and Back.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Credits</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Plays the credits from the first page, with their music.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Good Ending</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Plays the ending as if you had all six Chaos Emeralds.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Bad Ending</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Plays the ending as if you had none.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Wrecked Ship</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Switches Robotnik's wrecked ship in the distant background of the ending on or off.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Back</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Returns to the sound test (so does <guibutton>B</guibutton>).</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<note><para>Choosing an ending sets your emeralds to six or none; they stay that way until you start a level from
the title screen.</para></note>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<chapter id="tools">
<title>Under the Hood: The Debug Tools</title>

<para>These tools are only available while the debug cheat is active (always, in a debug build). Opening one of
them from the <guimenu>View</guimenu> or <guimenu>Tools</guimenu> menu opens a window of its own; the status bar
names every tool that is in use.</para>

<sect1 id="view-menu">
<title>The View menu</title>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>VDP Viewer</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>The video memory as tiles, the four palettes and the sprites, updated as the game runs.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Sound Viewer</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>The registers of the YM2612 FM chip and the SN76489 sound chip: key states, algorithms, frequencies,
levels and routings.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Console</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>A drop-down console (the <keycap>`</keycap> key also opens it) for reading and setting the game's
variables and running commands. The game is frozen while it is open.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Variables</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>The game's global variables, which you can edit.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Logging</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Starts and stops the event log, chooses its file and shows it in a window.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Object RAM</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>The object slots: type, routine, position and speed of each.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="tools-menu">
<title>The Tools menu</title>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Record Demo...</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Asks for a level (or a special stage) and records your button presses to a file, with a record
of where it started. <guimenuitem>Stop Recording</guimenuitem> saves it and carries on playing.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Play Demo...</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Plays a recorded demo back in its level.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>SMPS Inspector</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>See <xref linkend="smps-inspector"/>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="smps-inspector">
<title>The SMPS Inspector</title>

<para>The SMPS Inspector shows what the sound engine is doing, as it does it, and lets you play any song or sound
effect.</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The SMPS Inspector</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="screenshots/smps-inspector.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The SMPS Inspector: a row of piano keys for each channel, with the struck notes lit, and the drum kit below.</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>The game is frozen while the Inspector is open, but the sound engine keeps running, so that nothing from the
game can start a sound that competes with the one you chose.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>The sound list</term>
<listitem><para>Every song and sound effect, by its sound test number and name. Choosing one plays it
(<guibutton>&lt;</guibutton>, <guibutton>&gt;</guibutton>, <keycap>Left</keycap> and <keycap>Right</keycap> step through
the list). <guibutton>Stop</guibutton> silences everything.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>JSON engine</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Plays the sound through the JSON tree-walking engine (the one the level select's sound test uses)
instead of the byte-code driver the game itself uses. Comparing the two is how timing differences were found.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>The channel strips</term>
<listitem><para>A piano keyboard for each FM and PSG tone channel that the song uses, with the note being played
lit. A channel that drives the noise generator shows a row of squares instead, since its notes pick the character
of the noise.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>The drum kit</term>
<listitem><para>All ninety-five drums of the ParadoxSMPS drum kit. A drum lights when the song hits it, and
clicking one plays it by itself.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<chapter id="reference">
<title>Handy Reference</title>

<sect1 id="command-line">
<title>Command line options</title>

<para>These options are for testing and are not needed to play. They can be combined.</para>

<informaltable>
<tgroup cols="2">
<thead>
<row><entry>Option</entry><entry>Effect</entry></row>
</thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry><option>--zone</option> <replaceable>N</replaceable> [<option>--act</option> <replaceable>N</replaceable>]</entry><entry>Skip the title screen and start in that level: 0 Green Hill, 1 Labyrinth, 2 Marble, 3 Star Light, 4 Spring Yard, 5 Scrap Brain.</entry></row>
<row><entry><option>--x</option> <replaceable>N</replaceable> <option>--y</option> <replaceable>N</replaceable></entry><entry>Override Sonic's start position (with <option>--zone</option>).</entry></row>
<row><entry><option>--special</option> <replaceable>N</replaceable></entry><entry>Start in special stage N (0 to 5).</entry></row>
<row><entry><option>--ending</option> <replaceable>N</replaceable> [<option>--ship</option>]</entry><entry>Start in the ending holding N emeralds (6 is the good ending); <option>--ship</option> shows the wrecked ship.</entry></row>
<row><entry><option>--credits</option> <replaceable>N</replaceable> [<option>--emeralds</option> <replaceable>N</replaceable>]</entry><entry>Start in the credits at page N (0 to 9), holding some emeralds (6 shows END, fewer TRY AGAIN).</entry></row>
<row><entry><option>--resolution</option> <replaceable>N</replaceable></entry><entry>The picture shape: 0 original, 1 16:9, 2 8:5, 3 5:4, 4 4:3. Not saved.</entry></row>
<row><entry><option>--demo</option> <replaceable>FILE</replaceable></entry><entry>Play the level as a demo driven by the recorded input in FILE (with <option>--zone</option>).</entry></row>
<row><entry><option>--no-debug</option></entry><entry>In a debug build, start without the debug cheat (so the HUD is the normal one).</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</informaltable>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="settings-file">
<title>The settings file</title>
<para>Your choices (audio switches, volumes and muted channels, full screen, the window's size and position, the
picture's shape, the demo folder) are saved in
<filename>~/.local/share/SonicPC/Sonic1Settings.cfg</filename>, a YAML file that is rewritten as things change.
Delete it to start over with the defaults.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="troubleshooting">
<title>Troubleshooting</title>

<qandaset>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>The picture has black bars.</para></question>
<answer><para>The window is a different shape from the picture. Resize it, or choose the resolution that matches your
screen. Full screen always keeps the picture's shape.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>There is no sound.</para></question>
<answer><para>Check that <guimenu>Audio</guimenu> &rarr; <guimenuitem>Music</guimenuitem> and
<guimenuitem>Sound Effects</guimenuitem> are on and that their volumes are up, and that the channels you want are
checked under <guisubmenu>Channels</guisubmenu>.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>The View and Tools menus are missing.</para></question>
<answer><para>They only appear while the debug cheat is active (<xref linkend="debug-mode"/>).</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>A new resolution does not seem to take hold.</para></question>
<answer><para>Outside a level it takes hold when the next screen starts; start a level or return to the title
screen.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
</qandaset>
</sect1>
</chapter>

